<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Bilateral tender gynecomastia, suppressed testosterone and gonadotropins, and low libido in a 40-year-old man three weeks after the last of a year of high-dose estrogen injections given without his free consent. Please confirm the cause, advise what further tests are needed, and advise on recovery, testosterone therapy, bone health, and surgery. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Soon, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Does the exogenous estrogen fully explain the gynecomastia and the hormone results, and what further tests are needed to exclude another cause?</item><item>How quickly should the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis recover, and how should we monitor it?</item><item>Is testosterone therapy, tamoxifen, or an aromatase inhibitor indicated now?</item><item>Is the raised prolactin significant?</item><item>How should we protect and assess his bone health?</item><item>When should surgery be considered if the gynecomastia persists?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Turing is seen at the request of Dr. Hallam, five weeks after the last of about a year of high-dose estrogen injections that were imposed on him abroad as the alternative to imprisonment. He did not consent freely and will not take them again. Breast enlargement began within a few months of the injections and became tender; libido and erections were lost over the same months. He has no testicular pain or swelling, nipple discharge, headache, visual disturbance, or galactorrhea. He has never used anabolic steroids, androgens, or other drugs linked to gynecomastia and has no liver, kidney, or thyroid disease. He has had no leg swelling, chest pain, or breathlessness. He runs 20 to 40 km a week. His sexual orientation was discussed only as he chose; it has no bearing on these findings. He asked for each step of the examination to be explained, and agreed to each. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Alan Mathison Turing was born on 23 June 1912 in a nursing home in Paddington, London. [D]</item><item>He was a long-distance runner: he built his strength in cross-country running, was known for running to scientific meetings, and in 1951 was still training for long-distance running on the roads around Wilmslow, where he had bought his first house. [D]</item><item>After reporting a burglary at his home in 1952 he was arrested over a relationship with another man and tried on 31 March 1952 for &quot;gross indecency&quot;; rather than go to prison he accepted, for a year, oestrogen treatment intended to neutralise his libido. [D]</item><item>His biographer Andrew Hodges writes that the drug was probably stilboestrol, a synthetic oestrogen; the 2009 government statement and other sources describe the treatment as a series of injections of female hormones. [D]</item><item>Hodges describes the injurious and humiliating effect of the hormones on his body; it is often repeated that the treatment made him grow breasts, though he is known to have mentioned this only once. [D]</item><item>In a letter of 23 November 1952 he wrote that he had decided to have &quot;another, and rather more co-operative go at a psychiatrist&quot;; this was Jungian analysis with a refugee analyst, and he became very enthusiastic about it and wrote down all his dreams. [D]</item><item>His conviction led to the removal of his security clearance, so that he could no longer work for Government Communications Headquarters as he had since the war. [D]</item><item>After the trial he returned to a wide range of academic work, including morphogenesis, quantum theory, and relativity, at Manchester University. [D]</item><item>The roster lists depression as a primary condition; the sources read document his prosecution, the hormone treatment, and his decision to seek Jungian analysis, but record no diagnosis of depression, so a depressive illness is a retrospective interpretation. [R]</item></list><paragraph>5 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

