  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Bilateral gynecomastia (N62). True gynecomastia, typical on examination and fully explained by a year of high-dose exogenous estrogen. hCG and AFP are normal and the scrotal ultrasound shows no mass, so a testicular tumor is excluded; liver, kidney, and thyroid disease are excluded. Breast imaging is not needed because the examination is typical and shows no discrete mass. Most gynecomastia of recent onset regresses once the cause is removed, though tissue present for many months may become fibrous and persist. [S]</item><item>Drug-induced hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (E23.1). Suppression of LH, FSH, and testosterone by exogenous estrogen, with estradiol already falling. Recovery is expected over two to six months. Testosterone therapy would suppress the axis again and is not indicated for a transient, drug-induced deficiency. Tamoxifen and aromatase inhibitors are not justified in general and are not needed here. The mildly raised prolactin is an expected estrogen effect and should be rechecked after the estrogen clears; no pituitary imaging is needed unless it stays raised. [S]</item><item>Adverse effects of high-dose estrogen given without free consent (T38.5X5A). An iatrogenic harm. He has no symptoms or signs of venous thrombosis, and his liver tests are normal. The treatment had no medical indication. [S]</item><item>Vitamin D insufficiency (E55.9). Appropriate to replace while testosterone is low. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: The intake results, today&apos;s estradiol of 46 pg/mL and LH of 0.9 mIU/mL, and today&apos;s scrotal ultrasound, which shows testes of about 15 mL each, homogeneous, with no mass and no varicocele. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>No further estrogen; watchful waiting for the gynecomastia. [S]</item><item>No testosterone therapy, tamoxifen, or aromatase inhibitor while the axis recovers. [S]</item><item>Testosterone, estradiol, LH, FSH, prolactin, and hemoglobin about six weeks from now, then every six to eight weeks until normal; testosterone and estradiol once more at about six months. [S]</item><item>Continue cholecalciferol 1,000 units daily, calcium from food, and running with strength work; a bone density scan only if hypogonadism persists beyond six months or a fragility fracture occurs. [S]</item><item>Breast imaging only if the examination becomes equivocal or a discrete mass appears. [S]</item><item>A plastic surgery opinion if the gynecomastia persists about twelve months after the estrogen stopped and he wants it removed. [S]</item><item>Return to endocrinology if testosterone remains low or LH and FSH have not recovered six months after the last dose, or if prolactin stays raised. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Hallam the same day; Mr. Turing agrees with the plan and has a written copy. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Primary care monitors recovery. Endocrinology is available for review on the criteria above. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
